  "live_records": {
    "q-137739": {
      "dom_id": "step-q137739",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10$ and $5.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
